          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have both asthma and reflux and my cough is worse at night. Which is more likely to be driving it, and what does guidance describe for each?",
          "answer": "The sources I found do not say which condition is more likely to be causing your night cough. That would need a clinician who knows your history and can examine you.\n\n**What the asthma sources say** (Asthma, general clinical reference; Asthma diagnosis guideline): Asthma often causes wheeze, breathlessness, chest tightness and cough that are worse at night or in the early morning. Cough alone, especially at night, is a recognized way asthma can show up. Doctors confirm asthma with a breathing test (spirometry) and by checking whether airflow varies over time. A normal test on a good day does not rule it out.\n\n**What the reflux sources say** (GERD, general clinical reference): The typical reflux symptoms are heartburn and acid coming back into the throat. These are often worse after meals, when bending forward, and when lying down. Chronic cough is listed as a less typical sign of reflux. The source notes that cough from reflux is less specific, that many people with it turn out to have another cause, and that it responds less predictably to acid-reducing treatment.\n\n**What this means for you:** Both conditions can cause night-time cough, and the sources do not rank them for your case. Night-time cough is a recognized feature of asthma. Reflux is a less typical cause of cough, and the sources say it is often not the explanation. Asking a clinician to check whether your asthma is well controlled, and whether reflux is contributing, is a reasonable next step. Tell them that the cough is worse at night and when you lie down.\n\nIf you have trouble breathing, wheezing that is getting worse, chest tightness, or lips or fingers turning blue or grey, seek urgent medical care now. The symptom check found no emergency pattern in your description, but that check only covers a fixed list of presentations.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
